  "content": {
    "id": "p23-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Consider the equations $12x^2-4xy+11y^2=64$ and $16x^2-9xy+11y^2=78$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p23-q9-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "By letting $y=mx$, show that $7m^2+12m-4=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$7m^2+12m-4=0$.",
          "worked": "If $x=0$, the original equations would give both $11y^2=64$ and $11y^2=78$, so $x\\ne0$. Let $y=mx$. The equations become:\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2(12-4m+11m^2)&=64\\\\x^2(16-9m+11m^2)&=78\\end{align*}$$\nMultiplying the first by $39$ and the second by $32$, then equating:\n$$\\begin{align*}39(12-4m+11m^2)&=32(16-9m+11m^2)\\\\468-156m+429m^2&=512-288m+352m^2\\\\77m^2+132m-44&=0\\\\7m^2+12m-4&=0\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 71
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q9-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, or otherwise, solve the two equations simultaneously.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p23-q9-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "(x,y)=(1,-2),\\ (-1,2),\\ \\left(\\frac73,\\frac23\\right)\\text{ or }\\left(-\\frac73,-\\frac23\\right)",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p23-q9-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Factorise the quadratic in m, then substitute each ratio into an original equation.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Factorising the result from part a:\n$$\\begin{align*}7m^2+12m-4&=0\\\\(7m-2)(m+2)&=0\\\\m&=\\frac27\\text{ or }-2\\end{align*}$$\nFor $m=-2$, $y=-2x$. Substituting into the first original equation:\n$$\\begin{align*}12x^2-4x(-2x)+11(-2x)^2&=64\\\\64x^2&=64\\\\x^2&=1\\\\x&=\\pm1\\end{align*}$$\nThis gives $(x,y)=(1,-2)$ or $(-1,2)$.\n\nFor $m=\\frac27$, $y=\\frac27x$. Substituting:\n$$\\begin{align*}12x^2-4x\\left(\\frac27x\\right)+11\\left(\\frac27x\\right)^2&=64\\\\\\frac{576}{49}x^2&=64\\\\x^2&=\\frac{49}{9}\\\\x&=\\pm\\frac73\\end{align*}$$\nThis gives $(x,y)=(\\frac73,\\frac23)$ or $(-\\frac73,-\\frac23)$. Each pair satisfies both original equations.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 99
      }
    ]
